1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Today we revise Chapter Three.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,733
Data representation.

3
00:00:03,733 --> 00:00:09,408
How numbers, integers, and characters are shown in binary inside a computer.

4
00:00:09,758 --> 00:00:12,542
Three main lines in this chapter.

5
00:00:12,542 --> 00:00:14,904
One: number-base conversion.

6
00:00:14,904 --> 00:00:17,351
Two: integer representations.

7
00:00:17,351 --> 00:00:19,545
Three: character encoding.

8
00:00:19,545 --> 00:00:23,510
Remember: everything ends up as zeros and ones.

9
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,334
For each digit, keep two ideas apart.

10
00:00:26,334 --> 00:00:28,473
Face value is the symbol itself.

11
00:00:28,473 --> 00:00:33,354
Place value is the numerical value that digit represents in its position.

12
00:00:33,354 --> 00:00:36,229
Do not confuse face value with place value.

13
00:00:36,229 --> 00:00:46,324
For example, binary one zero one: the rightmost one times two to the zero; the middle zero times two to the one; the leftmost one times two to the two.

14
00:00:46,674 --> 00:00:49,851
Binary to hexadecimal follows the golden rule.

15
00:00:49,851 --> 00:00:55,237
From the radix point, or from the rightmost end, group every four binary bits.

16
00:00:55,237 --> 00:00:59,312
If a group has fewer than four bits, pad zeros on the left.

17
00:00:59,312 --> 00:01:04,146
Map each group to one hexadecimal digit: zero to nine, or A through F.

18
00:01:04,496 --> 00:01:06,814
Remember the storage unit scale.

19
00:01:06,814 --> 00:01:08,770
One byte equals eight bits.

20
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:13,623
One K B equals one thousand and twenty-four bytes — two to the ten.

21
00:01:13,623 --> 00:01:18,549
One M B equals one thousand and twenty-four K B — two to the twenty.

22
00:01:18,549 --> 00:01:23,475
One G B equals one thousand and twenty-four M B — two to the thirty.

23
00:01:23,475 --> 00:01:28,328
One T B equals one thousand and twenty-four G B — two to the forty.

24
00:01:28,678 --> 00:01:32,116
Integers have three common representations.

25
00:01:32,116 --> 00:01:33,555
Unsigned integers.

26
00:01:33,555 --> 00:01:35,074
Sign-and-magnitude.

27
00:01:35,074 --> 00:01:36,434
Two's complement.

28
00:01:36,434 --> 00:01:42,670
Focus on the most significant bit, the M S B, the zero problem, and the range.

29
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:47,043
In unsigned integers, every bit represents magnitude.

30
00:01:47,043 --> 00:01:48,789
There are no negatives.

31
00:01:48,789 --> 00:01:52,736
Eight-bit range: zero to two hundred and fifty-five.

32
00:01:52,736 --> 00:01:56,076
For n bits: zero to two to the n, minus one.

33
00:01:56,426 --> 00:02:03,665
Sign-and-magnitude: the most significant bit is zero for positive, one for negative; the other bits hold magnitude.

34
00:02:03,665 --> 00:02:06,876
The problem is dual zero: plus zero and minus zero.

35
00:02:06,876 --> 00:02:12,478
Eight-bit range: minus one hundred and twenty-seven to plus one hundred and twenty-seven.

36
00:02:12,478 --> 00:02:15,122
Do not confuse this with two's complement.

37
00:02:15,472 --> 00:02:16,549
Two's complement.

38
00:02:16,549 --> 00:02:21,174
The most significant bit is likewise zero for positive, one for negative.

39
00:02:21,174 --> 00:02:24,342
But there is a unique zero — all zeros means zero.

40
00:02:24,342 --> 00:02:29,981
Eight-bit range: minus one hundred and twenty-eight to plus one hundred and twenty-seven.

41
00:02:29,981 --> 00:02:34,480
Compared with sign-and-magnitude, the negative end has one extra value.

42
00:02:34,830 --> 00:02:39,667
To get the eight-bit two's complement of a negative integer, three steps.

43
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:43,311
One: write the binary of the matching positive integer.

44
00:02:43,311 --> 00:02:46,823
Two: invert every bit — that is the ones' complement.

45
00:02:46,823 --> 00:02:49,871
Three: add one — that yields two's complement.

46
00:02:49,871 --> 00:02:59,214
Example, minus twenty-nine: twenty-nine is zero zero zero one one one zero one; invert and add one to get one one one zero zero zero one one.

47
00:02:59,564 --> 00:03:03,908
Overflow error: the result falls outside the range that bit width can hold.

48
00:03:03,908 --> 00:03:10,336
Two's complement addition rule: if two same-sign numbers add to the opposite sign, overflow must have occurred.

49
00:03:10,336 --> 00:03:16,417
Positive plus positive yielding negative — overflow; negative plus negative yielding positive — overflow.

50
00:03:16,417 --> 00:03:19,892
One positive and one negative added together never overflow.

51
00:03:20,242 --> 00:03:22,987
Start character encoding with A S C I I.

52
00:03:22,987 --> 00:03:28,134
One byte, practically seven bits — one hundred and twenty-eight characters.

53
00:03:28,134 --> 00:03:31,771
Letters, digits, punctuation, and control characters.

54
00:03:31,771 --> 00:03:37,810
The leftmost bit is padded with zero into one byte; it is the base of Western encodings.

55
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:44,598
Big Five: one to two bytes; traditional Chinese common characters, and it includes A S C I I.

56
00:03:44,598 --> 00:03:50,691
A regional traditional encoding; no simplified Chinese; cross-region mojibake is common.

57
00:03:50,691 --> 00:03:53,391
G B code centres on simplified Chinese.

58
00:03:53,391 --> 00:04:01,698
G B two three one two is mainly simplified; G B K and G B one eight zero three zero are extensions with more characters.

59
00:04:01,698 --> 00:04:08,760
Do not claim every G B variant excludes traditional; traditional Chinese still commonly uses Big Five.

60
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:12,215
Unicode — commonly stored as U T F eight.

61
00:04:12,215 --> 00:04:18,500
Variable length, one to four bytes; covers the world’s scripts, symbols, and emoji.

62
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:25,921
Backward compatible with A S C I I: A S C I I characters take one byte; Chinese often takes three.

63
00:04:25,921 --> 00:04:29,102
It is also a common H T M L five standard.

64
00:04:29,452 --> 00:04:30,931
Remember four points.

65
00:04:30,931 --> 00:04:37,906
Do not confuse place value with face value; convert bases with place value and groups of four bits.

66
00:04:37,906 --> 00:04:46,783
Sign-and-magnitude has dual zero; two's complement has a unique zero, and eight bits reach minus one hundred and twenty-eight.

67
00:04:46,783 --> 00:04:50,024
Same-sign add to opposite sign means overflow.

68
00:04:50,024 --> 00:04:53,476
Encodings: A S C I I, Big Five, G B, and Unicode.

